\fs96 \cf2 Mojo structs\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A struct is Mojo's primary way to define your own type. When you want to model both data and behavior\'97whether that's a small value type, a numeric abstraction, or the foundation of a larger system\'97you'll use a struct.\
At a high level, a Mojo struct lets you bundle data together with the operations that act on that data. This makes structs a natural way to represent concepts in your program, rather than passing loosely related values through functions.\
Each Mojo 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 struct
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a data structure that lets you encapsulate fields and methods to store and operate on data. Structs can define the following members:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Fields are variables that store data relevant to the struct.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Methods are functions defined in a struct that normally act upon the field data.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Static methods are functions provided by the type to perform behaviors, provide constants, or create specialized instances.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Dunder methods are named for their _d_ouble _under_scored form, with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  on both sides. Also called "special methods", {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/structs/#special-methods"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 they help define behaviors}} such as initialization and allow structs to conform to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 traits}}.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members enable compile-time references that can be used for optimization.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For example, if you're building a graphics program, you can use a struct to define an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Image
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that has fields to store information about each image (such as its component pixels) and methods that perform actions on it (such as rotating the image).\
Mojo's struct format is designed to provide a static, memory-safe data structure that's both powerful and performant. Unlike dynamic objects (such as Python classes) that can be modified freely at runtime, structs are defined at compile time, which allows Mojo to generate highly optimized code.\
All struct fields must be declared using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and include a type annotation. This requirement is part of Mojo's compile-time guarantees, helping ensure both performance and memory safety.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Struct definition\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can define a simple struct called 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyPair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with two fields like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPair\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  first\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  second\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 However, you can't instantiate this struct because it has no constructor method. So here it is with a constructor to initialize the two fields:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPair\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  first\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  second\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  first\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  second\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 first \cf5 =\cf14  first\cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 second \cf5 =\cf14  second\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Notice that the first argument in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method is 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . You'll have a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument as the first argument on all struct methods. It references the current struct instance (it allows code in the method to refer to "itself"). When you call the constructor, you never pass a value for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \'97Mojo passes it in automatically.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  portion of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#argument-conventions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 argument convention}} that declares 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as a mutable reference that starts out as uninitialized and must be initialized before the function returns.\
Many types use a field-wise constructor like the one shown for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyPair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  above: it takes an argument for each field, and initializes the fields directly from the arguments. To save typing, Mojo provides a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/decorators/fieldwise-init/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul \ulc5 @fieldwise_init}} decorator, which generates a field-wise constructor for the struct. So you can rewrite the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyPair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  example above like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPair\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  first\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  second\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method is one of many \cf5 \ul \ulc5 special methods\cf2 \ulnone  (also known as "dunder methods" because they have double underscores) with pre-determined names.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb18 You can't assign values when you declare fields. You must initialize all of the struct's fields in the constructor. (If you try to leave a field uninitialized, the code won't compile.)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Constructing a struct type\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Once you have a constructor, with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @fieldwise_init
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you can create an instance of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyPair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and set the fields:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 Construct an instance\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  mine \cf5 =\cf14  MyPair\cf5 (\cf20 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf20 4\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 print(\cf14 mine\cf5 .\cf14 first\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 2\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Initializer lists make it easier to construct instances without spelling out full type names. Due to Mojo's type system, those names can grow long and complex as generic parameters, constraints, and nested types accumulate. Writing them out explicitly can quickly become verbose and hard to read. Initializer lists simplify code, improving readability and maintainability.\
For example, some machine-learning operations produce tensor view types whose fully spelled-out names can run to thousands of characters. Initializer lists let you construct these values concisely, relying on surrounding type context instead of repeating the entire type.\
Here's the standard way to call a function:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 Calling a function\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 process_pair\cf5 (\cf14 pair\cf5 :\cf14  MyPair\cf5 )\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13 process_pair\cf5 (\cf14 MyPair\cf5 (\cf20 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf20 4\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Because 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 process_pair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  expects a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyPair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instance, Mojo can infer the type from context. The braces construct the instance using an initializer list. Although the syntax uses braces, this is not a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Set
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . It is an initializer list used to create a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 MyPair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  value:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 Using an initializer list\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 process_pair\cf5 (\{\cf20 2\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf20 4\cf5 \})\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Initializer lists support all Mojo argument styles, including keyword arguments. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 Initializer lists with a keyword argument\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 utility_function\cf5 (\cf14 argument\cf5 :\cf14  CustomType\cf5 )\cf14  raises\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13 utility_function\cf5 (\cf14 CustomType\cf5 (\cf20 0.5\cf5 ,\cf14  fish\cf5 =\cf21 "swordfish"\cf5 ))\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 ...is\cf14  equivalent to\cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13 utility_function\cf5 (\{\cf20 0.5\cf5 ,\cf14  fish\cf5 =\cf21 "swordfish"\cf5 \})\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The result is concise, and both calls are functionally identical in the preceding example.\
You can also update declared instances with initializer lists. Mojo knows the variable's type. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf14 \cb19 Reassignment with initializer lists\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb13 # Construct a new instance\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb13 my_instance \cf5 =\cf14  CustomType\cf5 (\cf20 0.5\cf5 ,\cf14  fish\cf5 =\cf21 "swordfish"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # Use the instance\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # Update the instance before further use\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb13 my_instance \cf5 =\cf14  \cf5 \{\cf20 0.7\cf5 ,\cf14  fish\cf5 =\cf21 "salmon"\cf5 \}\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Mutating a struct\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 By default, a struct's methods receive an immutable 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , so they can't modify the struct's fields. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 struct MyStruct:\cb1 \
\cb13     var value: Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     def increment(self):\cb1 \
\cb13         self.value += 1 # ERROR: expression must be mutable in assignment\cb1 \
\
\cb13     # ...\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 To allow a method to mutate the instance, declare its receiver as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 mut self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . This makes 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  mutable inside the method and allows changes to its fields that persist after the method returns:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 struct MyStruct:\cb1 \
\cb13     var value: Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     def increment(mut self):\cb1 \
\cb13         self.value += 1 # Works: Mutable `self` allows assignment\cb1 \
\
\cb13     #...\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb18 Read more about mutable arguments and the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb22 mut
\fs28 \cf2 \cb18  keyword: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/values/ownership/#mutable-arguments-mut"}}{\fldrslt \ul Mutable arguments (
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cb22 mut
\fs28 \cb18 )}}\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Making a struct Copyable\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo structs are not copyable or movable by default.\
For example, the following code produces errors:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  a \cf5 =\cf14  MyPair\cf5 (\cf20 1\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf20 2\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # Implicit copy\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  b \cf5 =\cf14  a  \cf6 # value of type 'MyPair' cannot be implicitly copied,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13            \cf6 # it does not conform to 'ImplicitlyCopyable'\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # Explicit copy\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  c \cf5 =\cf14  a\cf5 .\cf14 copy\cf5 ()\cf14   \cf6 # value of type 'MyPair' cannot be implicitly copied,\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # it does not conform to 'ImplicitlyCopyable'\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # Move\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  d \cf5 =\cf14  a\cf5 ^\cf14   \cf6 # value of type 'MyPair' cannot be copied or moved; consider\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf6 # conforming it to 'Copyable', which also adds 'Movable'\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13             \cf6 # conformance.\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In most cases, you can make a struct copyable (and movable) just by adding the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul trait}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Movability\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To make a struct move-only, add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPair\cf5 (\cf14 Movable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo will generate a move constructor for you. You rarely need to write your own custom move constructor. For more information, see the section on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#move-constructor"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul move constructors}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Copyability\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To make a struct copyable, add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPair\cf5 (\cf14 Copyable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In most cases, that's all you need to do. Mojo will generate a copy constructor ( 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__(out self, *, copy: Self)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method) for you. You don't need to write your own unless you need custom logic in the copy constructor; for example, if your struct dynamically allocates memory. For more information, see the section on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/life/#copy-constructor"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul copy constructors}}.\
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait provides two ways to copy a value: the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 copy()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instance method and the copy initializer. In addition, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  automatically adds movability, so you won't need to add both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to your declarations.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Implicit copyability\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To make a struct implicitly copyable, add the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ImplicitlyCopyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPair\cf5 (\cf14 ImplicitlyCopyable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ImplicitlyCopyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  automatically implies 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , so all the notes related to copyability apply here. A type should only be implicitly copyable if copying the type is inexpensive and has no side effects. Unnecessary copies can be a big drain on memory and performance, so use this trait with caution.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Fields\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Fields store a struct's data. When you declare a field, it becomes part of the struct's memory layout. Because the compiler knows every field's type at compile time, it can:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Calculate the struct's exact memory footprint\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Ensure all fields are initialized before use\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Generate fast, direct access to field data\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Prevent changes to the struct's layout at runtime\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Fields share the lifetime of their struct instance. They are created when the struct is created and destroyed when the struct is destroyed. This model avoids dangling references and partially constructed objects.\
Outside of your struct implementation, you access fields with dot notation (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 my_struct.field_name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ). Within the struct, your methods access fields using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self.field_name
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ). Mojo knows each field's location at compile time, making field access direct and efficient.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Field requirements\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You must declare field members with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  in structs:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyStruct\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     value\cf5 :\cf14  Int \cf6 # Error. Missing `var` keyword\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  count\cf5 :\cf14  Int \cf6 # Yes\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Unlike local variables in functions, this requirement lets Mojo reason about a struct's layout and guarantees that its memory is safe and predictable.\
You must use unique symbols for fields, methods, or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  members. These all exist in the same namespace:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyStruct\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  count\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  count\cf5 :\cf14  String \cf6 # Error. Invalid redeclaration of `count`\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can re-use a struct member's name for an argument or method variable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyStruct\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  foo\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 use_argument\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 ,\cf14  foo\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14   \cf6 # Argument shadows field\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf14 foo\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # Prints argument value\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 use_local\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 ,\cf14  value\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 var\cf14  foo \cf5 =\cf14  value  \cf6 # Local variable shadows field\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf14 foo\cf5 ,\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 foo\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # Prints local, then field\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You must mark 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as mutable if updating a field value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyStruct\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  foo\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 update_foo\cf5 (mut\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  new_value\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 foo \cf5 =\cf14  new_value\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You must initialize fields within constructors, and not at the point of declaration.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyStruct\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  foo\cf5 :\cf14  Int \cf5 =\cf14  \cf20 10\cf14  \cf6 # Error: Unknown tokens\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 comptime\cf14  bar \cf5 =\cf14  \cf20 10\cf14  \cf6 # Yes\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/parameters/#comptime-members"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb12 \ul comptime
\fs32 \cb1  members}}\cf2  are compile-time constants (not fields) and don't occupy instance storage, so they can be initialized at the point of declaration.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Field conventions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Like other Mojo elements, fields normally adhere to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/modular/modular/blob/modular/v26.2/mojo/stdlib/docs/style-guide.md#code-conventions"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul certain conventions}}:\
You should use conventional naming for field members:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Prefer lowercase snake_case for field names (for example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 user_count
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 max_capacity
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Use descriptive names that indicate purpose, and not their type (for example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 error_msg
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  not 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 msg_string
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
For members meant for internal use or to maintain invariants, add an underscore prefix (for example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 _private_field
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
For boolean fields, use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 is_
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 has_
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  prefixes (for example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 is_valid
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 has_data
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Avoid single-letter names except for common mathematical conventions (such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 y
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 z
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for coordinates).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Methods\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In addition to special methods like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you can add any other method you want to your struct. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyPair\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  first\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  second\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 get_sum\cf5 (\cf14 self\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Int\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 return\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 first \cf5 +\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 second\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  mine \cf5 =\cf14  MyPair\cf5 (\cf20 6\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf20 8\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 print(\cf14 mine\cf5 .\cf14 get_sum\cf5 ())\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 14\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Notice that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 get_sum()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  also uses the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument, because this is the only way you can access the struct's fields in a method. The name 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is just a convention, and you can use any name you want to refer to the struct instance that is always passed as the first argument.\
Methods that take the implicit 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument are called instance methods because they act on an instance of the struct.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb18 The 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb22 self
\fs28 \cf2 \cb18  argument in a struct method is the only argument in a 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb22 def
\fs28 \cf2 \cb18  function that does not require a type. You can include the type if you want, but you can elide it because Mojo already knows its type (
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb22 MyPair
\fs28 \cf2 \cb18  in this case).\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Static methods\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A struct can have static methods. A static method can be called without creating an instance of the struct. Unlike instance methods, a static method doesn't receive the implicit 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument, so it can't access any fields on the struct.\
To declare a static method, use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @staticmethod
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  decorator and don't include a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Logger\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 @staticmethod\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 log_info\cf5 (\cf14 message\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf21 "Info: "\cf5 ,\cf14  message\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can invoke a static method by calling it on the type (in this case, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Logger
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ). You can also call it on an instance of the type. Both forms are shown below:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 Logger\cf5 .\cf14 log_info\cf5 (\cf21 "Static method called."\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  l \cf5 =\cf14  Logger\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 l\cf5 .\cf14 log_info\cf5 (\cf21 "Static method called from instance."\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 Info:  Static method called.\cb1 \
\cb13 Info:  Static method called from instance.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Structs compared to classes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If you're familiar with other object-oriented languages, then structs might sound a lot like classes, and there are some similarities, but also some important differences. Eventually, Mojo will also support classes to match the behavior of Python classes.\
So, let's compare Mojo structs to Python classes. They both support methods, fields, operator overloading, decorators for metaprogramming, and more, but their key differences are as follows:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Python classes are dynamic: they allow for dynamic dispatch, monkey-patching (or "swizzling"), and dynamically binding instance fields at runtime.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo structs are static: they are bound at compile-time (you cannot add methods at runtime). Structs allow you to trade flexibility for performance while being safe and easy to use.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo structs do not support inheritance ("sub-classing"), but a struct can implement {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul traits}}.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Python classes support class attributes\'97values that are shared by all instances of the class, equivalent to class variables or static data members in other languages.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo structs don't support static data members.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Syntactically, the biggest difference compared to a Python class is that all fields in a struct must be explicitly declared with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 var
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
In Mojo, the structure and contents of a struct are set at compile time and can't be changed while the program is running. Unlike in Python, where you can add, remove, or change attributes of an object on the fly, Mojo doesn't allow that for structs.\
However, the static nature of structs helps Mojo run your code faster. The program knows exactly where to find the struct's information and how to use it without any extra steps or delays at runtime.\
Mojo's structs also work really well with features you might already know from Python, like operator overloading (which lets you change how math symbols like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 +
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 -
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  work with your own data, using \cf5 \ul special methods\cf2 \ulnone ).\
As mentioned above, all Mojo's standard types (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , etc.) are made using structs, rather than being hardwired into the language itself. This gives you more flexibility and control when writing your code, and it means you can define your own types with all the same capabilities (there's no special treatment for the standard library types).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Special methods\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Special methods (or "dunder methods") such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are pre-determined method names that you can define in a struct to perform a special task.\
Although it's possible to call special methods with their method names, the point is that you never should, because Mojo automatically invokes them in circumstances where they're needed (which is why they're also called "magic methods"). For example, Mojo calls the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method when you create an instance of the struct; and when Mojo destroys the instance, it calls the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method (if it exists).\
Even operator behaviors that appear built-in (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 +
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 <
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ==
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 |
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and so on) are implemented as special methods that Mojo implicitly calls upon to perform operations or comparisons on the type that the operator is applied to.\
Mojo supports a long list of special methods; far too many to discuss here, but they generally match all of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel#special-method-names"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Python's special methods}} and they usually accomplish one of two types of tasks:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Operator overloading: A lot of special methods are designed to overload operators such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 <
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (less-than), 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 +
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (add), and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 |
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (or) so they work appropriately with each type. For more information, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/operators/#implement-operators-for-custom-types"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Implement operators for custom types}}.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Lifecycle event handling: These special methods deal with the lifecycle and value ownership of an instance. For example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  demarcate the beginning and end of an instance lifetime, and other special methods define the behavior for other lifecycle events such as how to copy or move a value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can learn all about the lifecycle special methods in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul Value lifecycle}} section. However, most structs are simple aggregations of other types, so unless your type requires custom behaviors when an instance is created, copied, moved, or destroyed, you can synthesize the essential lifecycle methods you need (and save yourself some time) using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @fieldwise_init
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  decorator (described in \cf5 \ul Struct definition\cf2 \ulnone ), and the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Movable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  traits (described in \cf5 \ul Making a struct copyable\cf2 \ulnone ).\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri960\partightenfactor0
